## Decoding the question
Two limbs. **"Write a note on the colonial and capitalistic perspectives"** asks for a compact explanation of both (see Answer 14 for a fuller version). **"Throw light on the thesis that development is the new name of coloniality in the context of emerging perspectives of gender"** asks you to **explain** the thesis, **apply** it to gender, and **weigh** it. The "emerging perspectives" are **decolonial and post-development feminism** — name Quijano, Lugones, Escobar and Mohanty.

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction

#### i) Definition
**Colonialism** was the formal rule of one country over another. **Coloniality**, a term of the Peruvian sociologist **Aníbal Quijano** (==2000==), refers to the **patterns of power** that colonialism created and that **continue after independence** — in the world economy, in racial and gender hierarchies, and in who controls knowledge.

#### ii) The argument
The colonial and capitalistic perspectives show how empire and markets reshaped gender for power and profit. The thesis that "**development is the new name of coloniality**" claims that development continues these patterns by other means. The thesis is **partly true** — development often reproduces colonial hierarchies — but development can also be **owned and reshaped** by the people it serves, especially women.

### 2. The colonial perspective
Colonial rulers justified empire as a "**civilising mission**", portraying colonised women as victims of backward cultures (**James Mill**, ==1817==; **Katherine Mayo**, ==1927==). They **codified** personal law in its most patriarchal forms — the **Punjab Laws Act** (==1872==) put custom before Islamic inheritance — recorded **land in men's names**, controlled women's bodies (the **Contagious Diseases Acts**) and criminalised *hijras* (==1871==). Nationalists, in turn, made women guardians of the **inner world** of tradition (**Partha Chatterjee**, ==1989==).

### 3. The capitalistic perspective
Industrial capitalism separated **paid work** (male, public) from **unpaid domestic work** (female, private). Capitalism depends on this unpaid **social reproduction** and on women as **cheap, flexible labour**. **Maria Mies** (==1986==) called the process "**housewifisation**": defining women as housewives so that even their productive work — like the lace makers of **Narsapur** — is paid little.

### 4. The link
Mies argued that **colonialism, capitalism and patriarchy** form one system of accumulation: colonies, nature and women are all treated as free or cheap resources. Colonialism was itself capitalist expansion, led by trading companies.

### 5. Coloniality explained

#### i) Quijano
**Aníbal Quijano** distinguished **colonialism**, which ended with independence, from the **coloniality of power**, which continues in a world economy centred on the West, in racial hierarchies, and in the belief that Western knowledge is universal and superior.

#### ii) Lugones
**María Lugones** ("Heterosexualism and the Colonial/Modern Gender System", ==2007==) argued that colonialism imposed a **European gender system** — rigid binaries, the male-headed household, heterosexual norms — on peoples with different arrangements. She called this the **coloniality of gender**.

### 6. Development as a discourse
Scholars date the "development era" from US President **Truman**'s speech of ==20 January 1949==, which named much of the world "**underdeveloped**". **Arturo Escobar** (*Encountering Development*, ==1995==) and **Wolfgang Sachs** (*The Development Dictionary*, ==1992==) argued that development is a **discourse**: it defines the South as lacking, makes Western experts the authorities, and turns local ways of life into problems to be fixed. This is the core of the thesis.

### 7. The thesis applied (1): the "Third World woman"
**Chandra Talpade Mohanty** ("Under Western Eyes", ==1984==) showed that development and feminist writing often portray the "**Third World woman**" as poor, ignorant, veiled and victimised — the same image the colonial gaze used to justify rule. The rescuer changes from the colonial officer to the aid worker. **Lila Abu-Lughod** ("Do Muslim Women Really Need Saving?", ==2002==) made the same point about the use of Afghan women's rights to justify intervention.

### 8. The thesis applied (2): experts, donors and debt

#### i) Outside expertise
Development priorities are often set by donors, consultants and international agencies — as policy was once set by colonial administrators. Local women appear as "beneficiaries" and "target groups", not as decision-makers.

#### ii) Debt conditions
**Structural adjustment** and IMF conditions give foreign lenders power over national budgets, cutting services women rely on (**Diane Elson**, ==1991==).

### 9. The thesis applied (3): extraction through supply chains
Global supply chains draw on **cheap female labour** in the South — garment workers, home-based stitchers, cotton pickers — while profits flow to firms in the North. The **Rana Plaza** collapse (Bangladesh, ==2013==) and the **Ali Enterprises** fire (Karachi, ==2012==) show the human cost. This mirrors the colonial extraction of raw materials and crafts.

### 10. The thesis applied (4): imposed gender models
Programmes may assume Western models of the family, of "empowerment" or of gender identity without asking local women — Lugones's coloniality of gender in a new form. Indicators designed abroad may count what donors value and miss what local women value, such as security within extended families or rights under religious law.

::: fig mapping | How development can repeat colonial patterns — the thesis applied to gender.
@left Colonial pattern
@right Development today
Civilising mission => "Saving" Third World women (Mohanty)
Colonial administrators => Donors, consultants and experts
Tribute and trade monopolies => Debt and loan conditions
Extraction of crafts and raw materials => Cheap female labour in supply chains
Imposed European gender system => Imported models of family and empowerment
:::

### 11. The counter-argument

#### i) Real gains
Development has brought **vaccines, schools, clean water**, lower maternal and child mortality and rising incomes — gains women value.

#### ii) Southern ownership
Southern thinkers shaped development: the Pakistani economist **Mahbub ul Haq** created the **Human Development Report** (==1990==); **DAWN** (==1984==), a Southern women's network, shaped GAD and Beijing.

#### iii) Women's own demands
Women in the South **demand** education, health, safety and rights. Calling these "colonial" risks silencing them and defending local patriarchy. Conservative opponents of women's rights often use the language of anti-colonialism to reject reforms that women themselves have demanded.

### 12. The Pakistan angle

#### i) Evidence for the thesis
Repeated **IMF programmes** (most recently a 7-billion-dollar facility in ==2024==) shape budgets and prices; many gender projects have been **donor-driven**; women work at the bottom of global **textile chains**.

#### ii) Evidence against
Pakistan's key gender reforms — the **MFLO** (==1961==), the **Protection of Women Act** (==2006==), the **honour-killing law** (==2016==) and the **child-marriage law** (==2025==) — were won by **Pakistani women's movements** and legislators, often argued in Islamic and constitutional terms.

### 13. Conclusion
The colonial and capitalistic perspectives show that gender has long been reshaped by **power and profit**. The thesis that development is the new coloniality captures a real danger: development can repeat the colonial gaze, outside control, extraction and imposed models. But it is not the whole truth. Development becomes emancipatory when it is **owned by local people — especially women** — and when it addresses **power**, not only projects. The task is to **decolonise development, not abandon it**.

## Why this answer scores
- **Both limbs** answered: a compact note on the two perspectives, then the thesis.
- The "emerging perspectives" are named and explained — **Quijano, Lugones, Escobar, Sachs, Mohanty**.
- The thesis is **applied to gender** in four ways and **weighed** against a counter-argument.
- A **mapping figure** and **Pakistani evidence** on both sides lead to a reasoned verdict.
